--TEST--
`Core\Uri::decodeFormValue` has exactly one refusal and the text cannot cause it: it answers for every one of the 128 raw ASCII bytes and for all 256 arrangements of a `%` with a non-hex neighbour, and where the octets do refuse it refuses the *whole* subject wherever the escape sits rather than answering the prefix it had already decoded — spec § 12, ADR 0009
--FILE--
<?nvs
// Two claims that the neighbouring cases each half-state. The malformed-escape
// case shows six rows decoding to themselves; the octet case shows five
// families throwing. Neither says that those are the only two outcomes there
// are, which is what makes this member safe to hand a query string a stranger
// wrote: no arrangement of `%`, `+` and text throws, and the one thing that
// does throw is a property of the octets the escapes spell.
//
// Both halves were checked against PHP 8.5 while this was written: `urldecode`
// answers the same text for every row below that Novis answers for, the
// refusals being ADR 0009's divergence, which the module doc owns.

// First: the raw bytes, unescaped. A `%`-free subject has no escape to be
// well formed, so every one of the 128 ASCII bytes is text — the `+` that is a
// space, the `%` that begins nothing, and the NUL a C string could not hold.
int $rawAnswered = 0;
int $rawByte = 0;
for (; $rawByte <= 127; $rawByte = $rawByte + 1) {
    string $character = Core\Str::fromCodePoint($rawByte as uint);
    try {
        var $answer = Core\Uri::decodeFormValue($character);
        $rawAnswered = $rawAnswered + 1;
    } catch (Throwable $rawRefused) {
        echo "raw byte ", $rawByte, " refused\n";
    }
}
echo "raw bytes 128, answered ", $rawAnswered, "\n";

// Second: a `%` that cannot begin an escape, swept. `Z` is not a hex digit, so
// `%<c>Z` and `%Z<c>` are malformed for every byte `c` — including the hex
// digits, including a second `%`, including a `+`. Every one is text, so every
// one is answered, and `decodeComponent` answers the subject back unchanged.
// A member that gave up at a `%` and resumed after it, or that repaired a
// half-escape, would still answer here; what it would fail is the count.
int $malformedAnswered = 0;
int $unchangedByComponent = 0;
int $decodersDiffer = 0;
int $neighbour = 0;
for (; $neighbour <= 127; $neighbour = $neighbour + 1) {
    string $byte = Core\Str::fromCodePoint($neighbour as uint);
    array<string> $arrangements = ["%" . $byte . "Z", "%Z" . $byte];
    foreach ($arrangements as string $row) {
        try {
            var $byForm = Core\Uri::decodeFormValue($row);
            $malformedAnswered = $malformedAnswered + 1;
            if ($byForm != Core\Uri::decodeComponent($row)) {
                $decodersDiffer = $decodersDiffer + 1;
            }
        } catch (Throwable $malformedRefused) {
            echo "malformed [", $row, "] refused\n";
        }
        if (Core\Uri::decodeComponent($row) == $row) {
            $unchangedByComponent = $unchangedByComponent + 1;
        }
    }
}
echo "arrangements 256, answered ", $malformedAnswered,
     ", unchanged by decodeComponent ", $unchangedByComponent,
     ", the two decoders differ on ", $decodersDiffer, "\n";

// Those two are the `+` in each position, which is the previous sentence's own
// bound: the one byte the form decoder rewrites is rewritten inside a malformed
// escape too, so it is not the escape scan that spares it.
echo "[", Core\Uri::decodeFormValue("%+Z"), "] [", Core\Uri::decodeFormValue("%Z+"), "]\n";

// Third: where the octets *do* refuse, the refusal is the whole subject. A
// decoder that wrote as it scanned would answer `head` for the second row and
// throw afterwards; this one produces a value or throws, never both. Each row
// is paired with the same text carrying a well-formed sequence in the same
// position, so the count on the accepting side says the position is not itself
// the reason.
array<string> $positions = ["%FFtail", "head%FFtail", "head%FF", "a+b%FF+c"];
array<string> $wellFormed = ["%C3%BFtail", "head%C3%BFtail", "head%C3%BF", "a+b%C3%BF+c"];
int $refused = 0;
foreach ($positions as string $bad) {
    try {
        var $partial = Core\Uri::decodeFormValue($bad);
        echo "answered [", $partial, "]\n";
    } catch (Throwable $wholeRefusal) {
        $refused = $refused + 1;
    }
}
int $accepted = 0;
foreach ($wellFormed as string $good) {
    try {
        var $whole = Core\Uri::decodeFormValue($good);
        $accepted = $accepted + 1;
    } catch (Throwable $unexpected) {
        echo "unexpectedly refused [", $good, "]\n";
    }
}
echo "positions 4, refused ", $refused, ", the same positions well formed, accepted ",
     $accepted, "\n";
echo "[", Core\Uri::decodeFormValue("a+b%C3%BF+c"), "]\n";
--EXPECT--
raw bytes 128, answered 128
arrangements 256, answered 256, unchanged by decodeComponent 256, the two decoders differ on 2
[% Z] [%Z ]
positions 4, refused 4, the same positions well formed, accepted 4
[a bÿ c]
